/** Translation maps for siman 042 — imported by _patch-siman-042.mjs */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  mechaber: {
    '1#main': `The laws of gall-bladder treifot. In it are 9 seifim. A gall bladder that was punctured — treifah. And if it was punctured opposite the liver and the liver seals it — kosher. {Rama: If the gall bladder adhered to another place — kosher (Mordechai Piskei Avodah Zarah and Isur ve-Heter HaArukh) (and see above siman 46).}`,
    '2#main': `Removed by hand or missing — treifah.`,
    '3#main': `If the gall bladder is missing, tear the liver in two and crosswise and taste it with his tongue; if he tastes bitter taste — kosher. And if he did not taste bitter taste, roast it on coals and taste; if there is bitter taste in it — kosher. And if not — treifah. {Rama: And even now we may rely on tasting the liver (Piskei Maharai siman 30).}`,
    '4#main': `If two gall bladders are found — treifah, as one removed is like its blood. And if there is one and it appears as two, puncture it; and if they pour into one another — it is one and kosher; and if not — they are two and treifah.`,
    '5#main': `If they punctured it and they were not pouring into one another, and afterward they inflated one and its fellow also rose in that inflation, or they put water into one of them and thereby its fellow also became filled with water — kosher.`,
    '6#main': `Even though they are two complete gall bladders, only that at the place of their adhesion to the liver, such as the width of a finger, they were as one — kosher, even if they did not pour into one another. And the same law if at their beginning they are two and at the end they become mixed and become one — kosher.`,
    '7#main': `If there were two gall bladders in the liver, one here and one here, and one duct pours bile to both of them; and when a person stands the animal upright, bile returns to the place where the duct divides bile and pours to both of them; but when they lie as they stand in the animal's life they do not pour into one another — there is to permit in an animal of Israel. {Rama: A gall bladder recognizable from side to side of the liver — if they pour into one another it is one and kosher (so explained the words of Mordechai that Isur ve-Heter HaArukh brought, general rule 52 dalet-tet); and if they do not pour into one another — treifah.}`,
    '8#main': `There are species of birds that have no gall bladder, such as turtledoves and young pigeons; and one does not forbid them since the entire species is so. And a deer has no gall bladder in the liver, but it has one below near its tail. {Rama: One who has before him a bird whose species he does not recognize and no gall bladder is found — one does not say that certainly its entire species has no gall bladder; rather we say that presumably it has a gall bladder until one knows that this species has no gall bladder (responsum Rashba siman 108).}`,
    '9#main': `A kernel found in the gall bladder: if it was like a date pit that has no sharp point — permitted; and if its point is sharp like an olive pit — forbidden, for behold it punctured it when it entered, and this is so the puncture not be seen because the mouth of the wound scabbed over. {Rama: And all the more if a needle or thorn is found in the gall bladder — treifah (Or Zarua, Ran, Rambam Hilchot Shechitah, Bach, Maharshal Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 49, and end of Sha'ar HaDin).}`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `A gall bladder that was punctured, etc. And Rashba wrote: where it is possible to attribute to a knife or the slaughterer's hand — we attribute, as above siman 36 seif 5 regarding lung; and Beit Yosef, and it is straightforward. And so the acharonim; and see Taz general rule 88 on laws of gall bladder.`,
    '1#ב': `And the liver seals it, etc. And if the liver too was punctured opposite it — we say in the Gemara (daf 48a) treifah; and so the poskim.`,
    '1#ג': `The gall bladder adhered, etc. — that is, as the Mechaber wrote siman 37 that adhesion disqualifies only in lung and not in other organs; and so below siman 46. And so Bach there. And the reason is that possibly in other organs it is not adhesion at all but only separation of moisture; and this is not like Maharshal Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 56, as written in books there.`,
    '3#א': `And taste it. And even though we establish in several places that it is forbidden to taste food to know if there is forbidden matter — nevertheless Maharshal wrote: when buying meat from slaughterers and it is not known if it is salted or not — forbidden to taste it with his tongue; and Bach brings it siman 96 seif 4. Possibly here the Geonim established that it is an uncommon matter that the gall bladder be entirely removed, and the norm is that it is absorbed in its place; and it is likely one will taste bitter taste; and so is implied from the words of the poskim.`,
    '3#ב': `Bitter taste — kosher. For they said: the liver is angry and the gall bladder throws a drop into it.`,
    '3#ג': `And even now, etc. See above siman 96 s.k. 5 and siman 98 s.k. 5.`,
    '4#_': `Puncture it, etc. As above siman 37 regarding two blisters that adhere to one another; and below siman 47 regarding two ducts of the intestines.`,
    '5#_': `Kosher. And what did not pour through its fellow when first punctured was on account of thickness of the moisture — Beit Yosef.`,
    '6#_': `Like the width of a finger. And even though below siman 47 we require a finger-width below and a finger-width above — there it is different because the food must exit; therefore it must be mixed below and above, which is not so here.`,
    '7#א': `There is to permit in an animal of Israel. Bach wrote: from Rashba's language it is implied that even in an animal of Israel one should forbid l'chatchila, etc.; and l'fachad — nevertheless the Mechaber understood Rashba thus; but since many poskim permit entirely with two gall bladders, it is reasonable to permit in such a case in an animal of Israel; and so Maharshal Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 12.`,
    '7#ב': `If they pour into one another, etc. And even though regarding lung above siman 37 seif 5 we say the opposite — that is on account of ducts; not so in liver, for even if the ducts were removed — kosher; and so Darkei Moshe; and it is straightforward.`,
    '8#_': `And the deer, etc. But in Sha'arei Shamayim that Maharai composed, sha'ar 10, he wrote that deer have no gall bladder — not considered as removal, for so is the entire species; and so is in Hagahot Sha'ar HaDin siman 88; and so Agudah Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 49: tradition that deer have no gall bladder; and so Or Zarua general rule 52 law 9.`,
    '9#א': `Kernels, etc. See above siman 46 seif 4 and what is written there.`,
    '9#ב': `And all the more if a needle is found, etc. And there is no distinction between a pouch and outside or inside — always treifah; and so Darkei Moshe; and so Maharshal Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 49 and other acharonim.`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#_': `The gall bladder adhered. The reason I wrote at end of siman 41 that we do not say "there is no adhesion without puncture" except regarding lung specifically.`,
    '3#_': `And even now, etc. For the lenient have multiplied regarding missing gall bladder; and they are fit to be relied upon, at least in such a case — so Darkei Moshe.`,
    '9#_': `A kernel found, etc. And even though it cannot exit through the duct — we say it entered the gall bladder via movement.`,
  },
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#_': `Seals it. And Shach wrote: but if the liver too was punctured opposite it — treifah; and Rashba wrote: where possible to attribute to knife or slaughterer's hand — we attribute, as above siman 36 seif 5 regarding lung.`,
    '2#_': `Adhered. We do not say there is no adhesion without puncture except regarding lung specifically; and see above siman 46. (Gall bladder and ducts that adhered to one another — kosher — Chayei Adam siman 37; and see Peri Chadash who disputes Rama. Gall bladder protruding between ribs outward between skin and flesh, and the gall bladder was found whole and they inflated the gall bladder and it rose in inflation and no puncture at all was found — Sefer Tzitz Eliezer siman 9 replied to permit; and see Peri Chadash.)`,
    '3#_': `Treifah. And Shach wrote: even though we establish it is forbidden to taste food to know if there is forbidden matter — nevertheless here they established that the matter is uncommon and it is likely one will taste bitter taste. (If there was no gall bladder and before tasting the liver was lost — kosher — Chayei Adam and Orach Chaim siman 16; and similarly Kenesset HaGedolah. Taste its place and bitter taste is found and taste again and bitterness is not found — we attribute that it was wiped off and it is kosher — Peri Chadash in name of Rikanti.)`,
    '4#_': `To rely. And the reason is that in any case the lenient have multiplied regarding missing gall bladder.`,
    '5#_': `Kosher. And what did not pour to its fellow when first punctured was on account of thickness of the moisture — Beit Yosef. (And this is when there is no taste of its bitterness in the liver; but if there is bitter taste — kosher per many poskim from law of entirely missing gall bladder — Peri Chadash; and in Sha'arei Yeshurun a question — general rule 52 disputes him. Rikanti wrote: an incident came to me — there was a blister like a small pouch coming out near the gall bladder hanging on the duct near it with a finger-width and they did not pour into one another; they punctured the blister and clear water came out; they tasted the water and it was not bitter; they permitted it, for it is not gall bladder but a blister; and there is no treifah of blister except in lung. Ahel Mo'ed wrote: gall bladder that was emptied and the pouch hangs in its place — kosher; we attribute that via the duct the bile was poured and not via puncture at all; and Peri Chadash wrote on this; and they inflate it; and if after inflation no puncture at all is found — there is to attribute that it poured via the duct, rather than to say a puncture in the membrane rose and is not visible.)`,
    '6#_': `Finger. And Shach wrote: and even though below siman 47 we require finger-width below and finger-width above — there it is different because food must exit; therefore it must be mixed below and above, which is not so here.`,
    '7#_': `Pour. And even though regarding lung above siman 36 seif 5 we say the opposite — that is on account of ducts; not so in liver, for even if ducts were removed — kosher — Shach.`,
    '8#_': `And the deer. Shach wrote: deer have no gall bladder and it is not considered removal since the entire species is so (Tosafot Chullin daf 112 and in Sefer Sha'arei Shamayim wrote; and some Bach that they have no gall bladder at all and have in intestines like dove and species of quail and sparrow-freedom; and some have gall bladder in stomach and some in ears; and deer and ram — their gall bladder is in the tail; and see Radak Hoshea siman 7). And if the liver was removed from that species — one must leave an olive's amount in the place where gall bladder should be in other species; and the reason: gemara says olive in place of gall bladder and did not say "at gall bladder"; and see Mateh Yehonatan siman 89.`,
    '9#_': `Needle. And Shach wrote: there is no distinction between pouch and outside or inside — Darkei Moshe, Maharshal, acharonim; and Peri Chadash disputes — see there.`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#_': `Mishnah Chullin daf 42.`,
    '2#_': `Statement of R' Yitzchak bar Yosef there daf 43.`,
    '3#_': `Rosh there on the mishnah; and so Rambam chapter 6 Hilchot Shechitah; and Tosafot; and Rashba; and Ran.`,
    '4#_': `Tur in name of Bahag.`,
    '5#_': `There in name of Ra'avan.`,
    '6#_': `Rosh there and Rambam there.`,
    '7#_': `Rosh there and Sha'arei Perushim; and we learn from law of two blisters that adhere to one another above siman 37 and from law of two intestinal ducts below in siman 47.`,
    '8#_': `Beit Yosef in name of an incident that occurred thus.`,
    '9#_': `Writings of Maharar Yisrael siman 266 (learned from siman 46) from law of thin membranes; and so above.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#_': `Adhered, etc. As stated above siman 37.`,
    '2#_': `Removed, etc. As above siman 40 seif 5.`,
    '3#_': `If, etc. As written in Berachot 61b: the liver is angry, etc.`,
    '4#א': `Found, etc. As written Niddah 58b.`,
    '4#ב': `And if, etc. As stated there and 57a.`,
    '5#_': `If they punctured it, etc. For thickness of the moisture delayed them.`,
    '6#א': `Even though, etc. As stated there 58b.`,
    '6#ב': `And the same law, etc. There they return and mix; and see Rosh there and Shach here.`,
    '7#א': `There were two, etc. Shach: (Likkut) 66 even though, etc., and 67 there were, etc. — we are lenient in such a case because Ra'avyah permits entirely in removal; and all the more in excess; and his proof: we say the liver was removed, etc., and therefore the gall bladder too was removed; and see Mordechai and Hagahot Ashiri chapter 88. Yeshu'ot Shemesh (end).`,
    '7#ב': `Gall bladder, etc. Even though regarding lung if they do not pour into one another it is treifah there on account of ducts — not so here, for ducts were removed and it is kosher; and so regarding lung if they do not pour into one another — kosher there — there is no prohibition except because they adhere to one another; not so here.`,
    '9#_': `For behold it punctured it, etc. All this is Rambam's language; and see Tosafot there entry "but olive," etc., and if so it is, etc.; and Ran also challenged; and further challenged: gall bladder is like a large duct — per that entry kosher; rather the reason is as Rambam stated; and the reason is that gall-bladder skin is thin and soft — we are concerned.`,
  },
  'kereti': {
    '1#א': `A gall bladder punctured, etc.; and if possible to attribute to something common — we attribute; so all poskim.`,
    '1#ב': `And the liver seals it — meaning a superior sealing that adheres between them; so is implied from Rambam's language; and if the liver was punctured opposite this puncture — treifah, as explained in the gemara.`,
    '1#ג': `Adhered, etc. — that adhesion is only in lung; nevertheless there is in any case a difficulty in this too; there is to settle — see above siman 40.`,
    '3#א': `Taste it with his tongue; and even if nothing is found from the cup — see Peleti on this, for it requires further study.`,
    '3#ב': `And if the liver was lost — the view of Gershon's work is to permit; and see Peleti what he wrote on this, for my view is to be stringent.`,
    '3#ג': `And even now. And that which it is permitted to taste forbidden matter — Shach wrote: mostly tasting in liver is recognizable and this is not so; and see Peleti what he wrote on this; and from his words it is not difficult to say via a gentile taster; but the world is accustomed to taste by oneself; and in my opinion one should search at the spleen of a bird and near it on the majority a very small gall bladder will be found; and in vain they declare many chickens treifah.`,
    '3#ד': `And even now: if the gall-bladder pouch exists and the moisture inside was emptied — Peri Chadash wrote in name of Ahel Mo'ed that it is kosher; and see Peleti what he wrote on this — proof for removal of gall bladder treifah.`,
    '7#_': `There is to permit in an animal of Israel; even though Rashba inclines to be stringent — see what Peleti wrote: for us it is kosher without hesitation.`,
    '8#א': `That they have no gall bladder — see Peleti what he wrote in explanation of Hagahot HaRosh.`,
    '8#ב': `Nevertheless in liver we require an olive where gall bladder should be — see Peleti what he wrote siman 41.`,
    '9#א': `Sharp point — see Peleti what he wrote to settle the words of Beit Yosef and Tur — see there.`,
    '9#ב': `The mouth of the wound scabbed over — if inflation helps in gall bladder see what Peleti wrote on this to settle Maharsha's difficulty.`,
  },
  'peleti': {
    '3#א': `And taste it, etc. This law the Rav Ba'al HaG wrote and after him most poskim followed; and the Rav Ba'al Peleti argued: what of it that it fell inside the liver — in any case gall-bladder skin hanging is a punctured treifah and the like; what do we judge here that we do not have before us? And he asked a great question. In truth I and my colleagues and my associate in Prague wondered, for we did not see among our fathers and instructing rabbis who stood on this; and therefore we ruled that it was a tradition in the hands of Bahag that gall-bladder membrane is thin membrane adhering to the liver; and when all moisture and everything inside went into the liver, it too adheres to the liver; and the liver by breath and power of the soul draws the membrane until it adheres to the liver and becomes one membrane with liver membrane; therefore if you search the membrane you will not find it, for it adheres to the liver; and so we would learn in many when we reached this law with students; and so is implied in Ra'avan siman 214. And it appears more to me than what was difficult to me than what the rabbis challenged Rabbenu Yehudah from that which "he pours his gall upon the ground" and Job still lived — what difficulty? And is it for this that the gall bladder was punctured? Cannot gall moisture spread within the liver duct as we say in the gemara and below siman 59? And from there the duct was punctured and kosher and gall spread through the whole body and everywhere in Job's cry; but for this the gall bladder was not punctured — see Peri Chadash s.k. 12 in name of Ahel Mo'ed: if the pouch was emptied it is kosher, for we say via duct and not via puncture it was emptied. And further difficulty to Ra'avyah and his supporters who permit removed and missing gall bladder — only punctured is treifah — difficult per one who says daf 25a; and impossible punctured alone and cut alone, for cutting is not like punctured except in removal — what did they challenge Rabbenu Yehudah from Job "he pours upon the ground"? If so evidently Satan did not puncture the gall bladder, for his soul was guarded — only the gall was cut; and it is obvious his bitterness was poured; and nevertheless he was not declared treifah from this; and strained that "upon the ground" is precise — if inside the body how does "ground" apply, for it is in his midst; rather he took the gall outside his body — "they pour upon the ground" — and this is removal of gall; and examine — this is proof for treifah of gall removal. And therefore it must be that they established treifah of gall is on account of bitter gall moisture inside the membrane; and the membrane is only a guard for that moisture and treifah depends on it; but membrane itself neither raises nor lowers — only guards lest bitterness spread in the body; therefore punctured membrane is treifah, for bitterness will spread and exit; therefore even if bitterness spread via duct and membrane exists — nevertheless treifah; and all the more if membrane was cut — at least bitterness spread. If so when Job poured his gall upon the ground it is obvious it was treifah; and do not answer from this that Bahag ruled: if gall is inside liver we do not look further at gall-bladder membrane, for it neither raises nor lowers treifah in the body; and in truth in Sefer Ahel Mo'ed one may say "was emptied" is not precise that nothing remained, only that it was lacking — and this is language of "was emptied"; and it teaches kosher, for we are not concerned for puncture — requires further study. But it appears more as Peleti stated; but not for his reason that Bahag was in doubt in law if gall removed or missing is kosher since gemara did not mention treifot or treifah — only punctured is treifah as Mechabrim wrote; and Bahag holds: if we doubt whether it had gall — behold safek treifah; and "establish the animal on its presumption of kashrut" — presumption of kashrut, for if it never had gall at all it has no presumption of kashrut; and on this we judge kosher or treifah; and how does presumption of kashrut apply? Even per R' Shimson who holds we follow majority — here it turned out it has no gall and majority does not apply; but when we know it had gall — then it has presumption of kashrut and we establish on presumption; and if so — do not answer; certainly in any case it is difficult to understand: because we sense a little bitter taste we judge all gall comes to liver and daily gall throws a drop; but because gall was not entirely lacking until no remnant remains — in truth animal and bird are before us and there is no gall — there is doubt whether removed from its measure kosher or not; and we establish on presumption of permissibility; impossible perhaps it was only lacking and never had presumption of permissibility; therefore if they tasted bitter taste in liver this is conclusive proof there was gall here and afterward removed; then since treifah is doubt we establish on presumption of permissibility and kashrut; not so if no bitter taste found — no presumption of kashrut and doubt is forbidden stringently; and this is clear and correct; and one whose mind is toward truth will concede.`,
    '3#ב': `If he tastes. And even though safek issur — forbidden to taste — Shach wrote s.k. 4: here they were lenient, for mostly they have gall and will sense bitter taste; and this is strained, for on the contrary it is uncommon; and I — fifty years I instruct righteousness to God's congregation — did not find literally twice in missing liver that bitter taste was sensed in liver; and so testified the gaon elder instructor Mahar"g in Gershon's work siman 52: liver lost before tasting — wrote they did not find so in liver; and so the gaon Ba'al Sha'arei Yeshurun; therefore requires further study to permit lost liver; and on Bahag's law we are distressed; especially no proof to permit from Bahag, for there it says only to taste — but not said via Israel; and possibly via gentile taster who does not impair his craft and is trusted for law; but in truth per what I wrote above — if so it is like somewhat siman 36: doubt whether will sense bitter taste or not; and if you say he will sense — perhaps gall was removed and kosher. But per Ribash siman 88: tasting is only rabbinic; and if so it is rabbinic doubt for leniency and forbidden matter is not established at all. And it is not similar to meat in doubt of salting that forbidden matter is established — unsalted — therefore Maharshal forbade; not so here forbidden matter is not established and it is rabbinic matter.`,
    '7#_': `There is to permit in animal of Israel. Bach challenged: Rashba's responsa that Beit Yosef brought wrote stam to be stringent to beware and did not write to permit in animal of Israel; and Shach s.k. 10 wrote therefore he permitted since many poskim permit entirely with two gall bladders. And he wrote well; still Bach's difficulty stands: even Rashba saw with his eyes there is reason to side; for in any case many permit in removed gall; nevertheless he wrote to be stringent; but this permission is per those who say excess is like entirely removed; but Rashba holds like removed and it is puncture — therefore all agree with two gall bladders treifah if somewhat connected — this is gall puncture and all agree treifah; and Rashba follows his view and there is no siding to permit; but for us who establish it is not puncture but like entirely removed — therefore for one who permits removed gall, all the more in excess; and there is siding here to permit.`,
    '8#_': `There are species of birds without gall bladder — one must infer from Hagahot HaRosh beginning Avodah Zarah in name of Ra'avyah: he infers from teaching olive in place of gall bladder and not "at gall bladder" — removed gall kosher; see there. And difficulty: there are species without gall bladder — how teach stam at gall bladder? Those without gall — what do they teach? Therefore it teaches in place where gall should be. And if one holds they truly have no gall — no olive needed in place of gall; per his view removed gall kosher; nevertheless we require olive — see what I wrote beginning siman 41. And it must be he holds like Rosh: olive measure specifically in ox and large rams per Rambam; and if so olive cited is in these species; and these species have gall bladder — we find only deer and ram among small game and turtledoves among small birds, not among large species; and see below siman 48.`,
    '9#א': `Like date pits without sharp point — permitted per Rambam and his supporters: regarding olive we are concerned because it has sharp point punctured at its base; not so date; if so even if not as large as date, since no sharp point — kosher. And this is Beit Yosef's intent: Tur wrote like Tosafot and Rosh; per Rashi he should have written only if like date without sharp point; and Rashi's wording is corrupted — must read Rashba holds like Rambam; and Bach attacked without noticing gemara; and Peri Chadash sought to align Beit Yosef another way — correct as I wrote. But with all this I did not understand: in previous siman seif 18 it says measure of adhesion is like date pit — these are Rashba and Tur; and see above chapter 88 s.k. 11; and it must be reconciled: if he holds sharp point and can puncture — one law; if sharp point can puncture — if not, no; what is Bach's difficulty that he need not write "like date pits" but only sharp point — for us whenever not like date it is called sharp point; and if he holds sharp point cannot puncture at all — they are two matters; if so even in date perhaps punctured and came; and that there it is permitted since no sharp point even though can puncture — resembles lung; therefore Tur's words are clear; and Bach attacked in vain.`,
    '9#ב': `Because mouth of wound scabbed over — from this implies somewhat inflation does not help in gall bladder like lung; but it appears Rashi holds it helps; and thereby Maharsha's difficulty is settled daf 49 in Rashi "olive splits" — Rashi holds one who permits lung permits here too; and Maharsha wondered who forces him — we are in liver here; and per my words it is settled: Rashi holds all difference between lung and liver — this is examination for him, not liver; therefore in cut lung where no examination he also holds distinguish pouch inside outside and bar; and as Rashi daf 48b entry "the law"; and see Rashba; and if so gall bladder that has examination resembles lung not liver; and Rashi ruled well both equal in law; and also what Maharsha wrote: since this gall bladder is stated for one who forbids — nevertheless he permits date; if so he could permit pouch outside, etc.; and why did Rashi write there entry "in every case treifah" — per my words it is settled: regarding gall bladder there is examination; if so two leniencies — examination and adhesion — permit what is not so in liver where only one; and examine.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `Kosher. For adhesion disqualifies only in lung, as Mechaber wrote above siman 37 and below siman 46; and so Bach ruled; and so Taz and Shach. But Yeshu'ot Shemesh Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 48 and 56 forbids adhesion in other organs; and in Taharat HaBayit siman 253 forbids even if adhered to wall — we do not attribute to wall — see there, etc. Shelah daf 74b entry "regarding" in name of responsum Mishpetei Shmuel (it is siman 130) ruled to declare treifah; and he too ruled there. But in responsum Tzitz Eliezer siman 9 he elaborated to dispute and upheld Rav's ruling that adhesion disqualifies only in lung — see there; and so upheld in responsum Chatam Sofer siman 76. And he wrote there regarding thin membranes of calf that adhered to one another: in this all agree kosher; for even in Taharat HaBayit he forbade only adhesion to wall or another place that is adhesion not in order that has no examination at all; not so these thin membranes that rest — even if adhered end to end it is not like lung, for lung goes up and down frequently; but in thin membranes that rest it is like lung-adhesion in order and kosher — see there; and see responsum Chinuch Beit Yosef siman 36; and as I wrote siman 46 s.k. 5.`,
    '2#_': `Missing gall bladder. In Sefer Chamudei Daniel he wrote: once a gall bladder was found whose skin was thick and inside white moisture and it is not bitter — treifah; end; and see Peri Megadim in Mishbetzot s.k. 2 who also doubted this law and declared treifah from doubt — see there; and requires further study whether tasting liver helps in such a case per Rikanti brought in acharonim in incident: small pouch was at gall bladder, tasted and was not bitter, permitted — not two gall bladders but a blister; if so here too perhaps main gall was absorbed in liver and this is only blister; or perhaps only where one is as required do we say so, not here. Further requires further study: if all moisture is sweet and only in part bitter taste — whether to permit in such a case — requires further study; and see s.k. following.`,
    '3#_': `The liver. See Sefer Bnei Chayya who wrote: bird liver partly crushed and somewhat cut and no gall bladder found; tasted liver — no bitter taste; tasted gizzard — bitter like wormwood; and some wanted to be stringent saying we have no examination other than liver; but Mahar"a HaKohen permitted from double doubt: perhaps this bitterness found in gizzard is from gall that rotted and stuck to gizzard and by handling was uprooted from liver and poured to gizzard; and if you say it came from elsewhere — perhaps halacha is removed gall kosher; and it is double doubt turning; and further because it was crushed and somewhat cut we attribute gall was in that crushed cut place; Rav Maharach in Navasht agreed for this reason that gall tore to gizzard. Per what Taharat HaShulchan wrote and Peri Megadim in Sha'ar HaDin letter he: reason kosher via tasting liver — even though pouch is lacking, meaning we say presumably created lacking and bitterness absorbed in liver since gall's action is "liver angry," etc.; or pouch entirely adhered to liver until not visible — see there this permission does not apply: for what helps attributing that via handling afterward bitterness poured to gizzard — nevertheless pouch is lacking; cannot say case where pouch never lacking only empty; and on this he says we attribute bitterness poured to gizzard; if so kosher even if no bitterness at all as Peri Chadash s.k. 12 and all acharonim. However if we resolve our doubt in previous s.k. and say even if only part moisture bitter and rest sweet — kosher — you find this law here where gall was sweet and not whole and we attribute there was also part bitter moisture and via handling poured to gizzard; especially since question came that part liver was missing and can attribute gall was in that missing part and absorbed in that piece; and if before us bitter taste would be found — end; per his method: he holds if liver lost before tasting — kosher as written below s.k. 5.`,
    '4#_': `And taste with his tongue. See responsum Zichron Yosef siman 4 who brought in name of Rashal: correct to rinse liver at tasting place from visible blood; and wrote on this it is only loss of Israel's money, for Rikanti wrote (brought in Ba'er Heitev s.k. 3): if tasted and bitter found and tasted again and no bitterness sensed — kosher; we attribute first licking removed all bitterness; and therefore concern perhaps he removes bitterness by rinsing and afterward will not sense bitter taste and will declare treifah improperly; therefore rely on Peri Chadash that here it suffices with ordinary licking — see there. And see responsum Shevut Yaakov chelek 1 siman 23 who wrote: that we taste liver is even if pouch too removed (meaning lacking — see Peri Megadim) — not like responsum Hilchot Ketannot — see there.`,
    '5#_': `And if not — treifah. Ba'er Heitev and what he wrote: in responsum Orach Chaim he permits if liver lost before tasting — already Peri Megadim seized on him: on the contrary his view is to declare treifah — see there; and see Sefer Bnei Chayya who wrote in name of Rav Maharach in Navasht to permit — see there; and see responsum Shevut Yaakov chelek 2 siman 66 who also wrote in name of Orach Chaim to permit; and so he ruled practically; and wrote further he stood on experience many times no bitter taste in liver and saw gall stuck in intestines and fat; and sometimes small gall from fattiness of bird and not visible; and sometimes bitterness poured via stripping intestines from them; therefore it seemed simple: if intestines and fat lost and afterward no gall in liver — attribute stripped with intestines — see there.`,
    '6#_': `And even now. See Shach: it is likely one will taste bitter taste; and for this reason Shevut Yaakov chelek 1 siman 23 wrote: if no gall found — permitted to roast liver on Yom Tov to taste, for we rely on majority — see there.`,
    '7#_': `We may rely. See responsum Chinuch Beit Yosef siman 86 middle of answer who wrote: nevertheless he admits one may not rely on women and the like, etc. — see there; and so responsum Zichron Yosef chelek 14 end siman 4; and see Nekudot HaKesef what I wrote on this.`,
    '8#_': `Kosher. Ba'er Heitev in name of Rikanti. And similarly wrote responsum Chinuch Beit Yehudah siman 56 — see there; and see what I wrote above s.k. 2.`,
    '9#_': `Species of birds. See Sefer Tiferet le-Moshe who wrote: those species and likewise deer and rams — if their liver was removed and only olive left in place of its life and not in place of gall — kosher, for essence of treifah is gall as Rashba brought Beit Yosef end siman 41; and possibly even if removed in place of its life — kosher; requires further study; end; see there; and see Peri Megadim beginning siman 41.`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `A) [Seif 1] A gall bladder punctured — treifah. And if gall bladder punctured and possible to attribute to knife or slaughterer's hand — kosher. Beit Yosef in name of Rashba. Shach s.k. 1. Kenesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Beit Yosef ot 1. Peri Chadash ot 1. Lechem Panim ot 1. Beit Lechem Yehudah ot 1. Kereti ot 1. Sha'arei Perushim ot 1. She'erit Menachem ot 1. Sha'arei Gedolah Machon ot 1; and wrote: because of its commonness Shulchan Aruch and Rav in hagahah omitted this law of puncture made after slaughter that is drawn and visible to all, as above siman 36 seif 5 in hagahah regarding lung found punctured — see there. Zivchei Ra'anan ot 118. Machatzit HaShekel ot 2. Erech HaShulchan ot 2. Chayei Adam general rule 17 ot 29. Toldot Zevi ot 1. Ananah siman 21 ot 2; and in his book She'erit Adam ot 2. Zivchei Tzedek ot 1. Turei Zahav siman 70 ot 3. Beit Yitzchak in Sha'ar HaPerushim ot 1. Beit Yosef in Amud HaMa'arav ot 1. Minchat Yitzchak in roots ot 1. Zivchei Tzedek ot 1; and see below siman 50 seif 1.`,
    '2#_': `B) And that which we permit to attribute to slaughterer's hand — how so? Slaughterer passed his hand with force or stripped with force as law of lung. Erech HaShulchan there. Ananah there and in his book She'erit Adam there. Zivchei Tzedek ot 2; and see above siman 36 seif 5.`,
    '3#_': `C) There. And if punctured opposite liver and liver seals it — kosher. And if liver too punctured opposite — treifah. Chullin 48a. Beit Yosef. Shach s.k. 2. Peri Chadash ot 2. Perishah ot 1. Lechem Panim ot 2. Beit Lechem Yehudah ot 2. Kereti ot 2. Taharat HaBayit ot 1. She'erit Menachem ot 2. Sha'arei Perushim ot 1. Zivchei Ra'anan ot 117. Machatzit HaShekel ot 3. Toldot Zevi ot 4. Ananah there ot 4. Chayei Adam there ot 21. Zivchei Tzedek there. Turei Zahav there ot 4. Beit Yitzchak ot 1. Minchat Yitzchak in roots ot 2. Zivchei Tzedek ot 4.`,
    '4#_': `D) There. And liver seals it — kosher. And if puncture not sealed even though near liver — treifah. Rambam chapter 6 Hilchot Shechitah law 6. Peri Chadash there. Lechem Panim there. She'erit Menachem there. Toldot Zevi there. Chayei Adam there ot 21. Minchat Yitzchak in roots ot 3. However if punctured below in place not adhering to liver and sealed via adhesion that adhered to liver — kosher like lung punctured and wall sealed it — kosher for us in complex and soft. Turei Zahav there ot 2. Ananah there ot 3. Zivchei Tzedek ot 5.`,
    '5#_': `E) And if gall bladder punctured opposite liver and liver sealed it and liver punctured opposite and sealing rose on it and sealed — even though superior sealing — treifah. Taharat HaBayit ot 1. Sha'arei Perushim ot 2. Machatzit HaShekel end ot 3. Ananah there ot 4. Beit Yitzchak there in Amud HaMa'arav ot 2. Minchat Yitzchak in roots ot 2. Zivchei Tzedek ot 6.`,
    '6#_': `F) There hagahah: gall bladder adhered to another place — kosher. And so Shulchan Aruch above siman 37 and below siman 46 — adhesion disqualifies only in lung — see there. And Taz wrote there ot 5: so we cite. And so Ananah there ot 5. Zivchei Tzedek ot 7. Nevertheless examination is required — remove adhesion gently and check by inflation; if skin whole without puncture — kosher; and b'dieved if not checked — there is to permit. Mishnat Zekenim ot 1. Zivchei Tzedek there; and see above siman 37 ot 131.`,
    '7#_': `G) Gall bladder protruding between ribs outward between skin and flesh, and whole gall bladder found, inflated it and rose in inflation and no puncture found at all — kosher. Tzitz Eliezer siman 9; and disputed responsum Mishpetei Shmuel siman 130 who declared treifah — see there; and Peri Chadash ot 3 agreed; and so Kenesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Beit Yosef ot 5. Lechem Panim end ot 3. Beit Lechem Yehudah end ot 3. Mishnat Zekenim ot 1. Zivchei Ra'anan ot 119. She'erit Menachem end ot 3. Toldot Zevi ot 5. Ananah there ot 6. Zivchei Tzedek ot 2. Turei Zahav there ot 6. Beit Yitzchak in Taharat HaBayit ot 1. Kometz Bikkurim in laws of other treifot general rule 5 ot 8. And how so? If skin whole from outside; but if punctured from outside side — there is to attribute to thorn from illness and declare treifah. Peri Chadash there. Lechem Panim there. Mishnat Zekenim there. Zivchei Ra'anan there. She'erit Menachem there. Toldot Zevi there. Beit Yitzchak in Taharat HaBayit there. Kometz Bikkurim there. Zivchei Tzedek ot 8; and wrote: and not like Taz there. And in our opinion who permitted: Zivchei Ra'anan wrote: even if punctured from outside — does not emerge from doubt, lest via illness; and therefore wrote: in place of Peri Megadim there is to be lenient — see there. However from rest of aforementioned poskim who are many it is implied: if punctured from outside in every case — declare treifah. And see below siman 49 seif 4 and our words there ot 35.`,
    '8#_': `H) And if skin not before us and we do not know if it was whole — we attribute to leniency from double doubt and kosher. Taharat HaBayit siman 49. Ananah siman 21 ot 6. Zivchei Tzedek ot 9.`,
    '9#_': `I) Gall bladder and thin membranes that adhered to one another — there is to permit per majority view; and do not compare their adhesion to lung adhesion where we say there is no adhesion without puncture. Chatam Sofer siman 76. Lechem Panim ot 3. Zivchei Tzedek ot 10; and see above ot 6.`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 42 seif 4) Found two gall bladders treifah. See Peleti who wants to permit if two gall bladders found via tasting bitter taste in liver — and his words are error, for excess is like removal; because nature cannot uphold and hold except one from that organ; and if two found — its power is not so strong to uphold and hold; and certainly both will be lost; if so all the more if it has three gall bladders — certainly one may say in them loss and absence after time; if so even if we tasted bitter taste it is like three gall bladders and all the more treifah; therefore Peleti's law is broken (and see in Peri Megadim).`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 42 seif 3) If gall bladder missing. In Sha'arei Yeshurun wrote: if gall bladder missing — say in thin membranes; and Beit Lechem Yehudah brought them. And if gall lost before tasting — permits in Sefer Beit Avraham.`,
    '2#_': `(There seif 8) There are species of birds without gall bladder. Rosh beginning Avodah Zarah wrote: some bird species have no gall bladder and one does not declare them treifah; nevertheless do not learn, etc., that every animal species is one — even if one coarse and one fine; but birds are divided in their species and one does not learn these from those; end; see there. And requires further study: he is against gemara beginning of Chullin (daf 70b) and Rashi and Tosafot there explicitly opposite — that geese and chickens are one bird species with turtledoves and young pigeons; but goat is not species of calf — one coarse one fine. And in birds it does not apply to say coarse and fine species — see there; and requires further study. And see in responsum Zichron Yosef on bird where no gall bladder found and no double doubt — stringent to forbid eggs, for we judge it certainly treifah that laid; hospital birth or birth — we say miracle unless eggs mixed in others nullified in majority as dry-in-dry law.`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `And the liver seals it. And see responsum Chinuch Beit Yosef siman 36: gall bladder adhered forcefully to flesh between ribs and appears as puncture there.`,
    '2#_': `Gall bladder adhered. Ba'er Heitev; and see above siman 37 s.k. 23 where I brought words of Chatam Sofer and what he wrote on this — see there; and what Ba'er Heitev wrote in name of Tzitz Eliezer — gall bladder protruding, etc.; and see what I wrote: for us there is not to permit in such a case.`,
    '3#_': `Liver ground. Machatzit HaShekel wrote: in Shulchan Aruch that mentioned tearing — so is in Bahag and Pesikta Zutarta and Ra'avan; and Machatzit HaShekel brought many rishonim and acharonim who did not mention tearing; and implies tearing is to include even if not bitter outside — if torn and taste helps because we say bitterness absorbed; and so I saw Jerusalem rabbis taste liver without tearing and if bitter taste sensed — permitted; and they have basis to rely. But in Taharat HaShulchan wrote: error to taste without tearing, for liver bitter outside is no proof it absorbed on contact, etc.; and Machatzit HaShekel wrote: since tearing mentioned in Bahag and Shulchan Aruch — concern to tear and taste; and b'dieved external tasting suffices; and see there: even if pouch not found — also kosher via tasting; and see there: wrote per main text in Bahag: if trace that pouch and gall moisture were and removed — also permit via tearing and tasting liver; and not like Ahel Mo'ed; and l'chatchila do inflation as Peri Chadash; if did not inflate — all who saw it whole suffices; and all the more if lost without inflation. If tasted its place and bitter found and tasted again and not found — we attribute wiped what liver absorbed from gall and kosher — so Peri Chadash in name of Maharikash; meaning tasted at gall place and bitter found there even though tasted liver afterward and no bitter taste in liver — we attribute wiped what liver absorbed; and this is "tasted its place and again tasted"; and not as copyists seem on matter; and it is straightforward: if tasted that very place that was bitter at first and now not bitter — we attribute wiped in first tasting; not like Mishbetzot who gave taste that liver absorbed bitterness. And this is not Peri Chadash's intent. Therefore even if afterward they taste liver again and no bitterness — permit; but per Taharat HaShulchan appears not sufficient with slight bitterness — see there. And appears to be stringent without Peri Megadim in such case — rishonim gave no such limit. If gall as required and tasted liver and bitterness found — Peri Megadim in name of Pri Te'arah: kosher and straightforward. If separated intestines from liver and threw them and afterward no gall in liver — kosher; attribute remained with intestines as above in name of Shevut Yaakov; and so Mohar"i of Prague in responsum manuscript. And in truth so explained Peri Chadash s.k. 4. If no bitter taste and tasted intestines and bitter — permit as Sefer Shevet Yehudah to Mohar"i — see there; and essence not like disputant. If no bitter taste in liver or intestines and roasted Ba'al Mechaber and bitter taste found — declare treifah. In responsum Orach Chaim they brought in Bnei Chayya: liver somewhat crushed and cut and no gall in tasting liver; tasted gizzard — bitter like wormwood — permit. Liver of calf that had gall as required but very white; tore gall and clear sweet water inside — Mishbetzot in this siman declared treifah from doubt. Machatzit HaShekel wrote he saw in letter of gaon Moharav Baruch Katz Rapaport av beit din Frankfurt incident came to him and he declared treifah; brought from Maharshal who permits in variations not mentioned in gemara; and nevertheless in missing rose he declares treifah — none on it; and similarly when sweet — no gall on it; and all this for those who declare treifah in missing rose; but one who permits there — same here; end. (Words are closed: in my opinion this is not general rule for one who permits missing rose; understanding is easy and I shortened.) If sweet gall and tasted liver and bitter — permit and attribute pouch drew water like bubble as Moharikash in incident: pouch found without bitter water at gall — kosher, not gall but blister (brought Ba'er Heitev; and what brought in name of Ribash is corrupted — must read Rikanti); and similarly its counterpart; and see Chinuch Beit Yosef siman 56. If liver lost before tasting — many acharonim wrote minority of minority taste bitter in liver; therefore not even permit from double doubt that Kenesset HaGedolah and Machatzit HaShekel wrote: in their regions birds found without gall — mostly find bitter taste in liver; and still half-half; and in such places permit in lost case from double doubt. If they ate liver and did not taste bitter and remembered and asked for gall and not found — declare treifah as Dvar Moshe in name of Mohar"i David; and see Kereti: search at bird spleen and near it small gall found; and in vain many chickens declared treifah — see there. And see Machatzit HaShekel: his view that even Maharshal admits women are trusted to check via tasting liver to say it is bitter, since no exertion at all; and not like responsum Chinuch Beit Yosef siman 86 (and what written there that Rama too admits — not so: Rama in Taharat HaKodesh general rule 46 disputes Maharshal on checking legumes — see there) who wrote: even household saw no gall found and went — woman working says she tasted bitter in liver — believe her. And so Zichron Yosef; and so appears from Dvar Shmuel siman 313. Gaon Mohar"m son of R' Betzalel in Sefer Vikuach Mayim Chayim wrote: matter placed on women and they themselves know we rely on them — then certainly check well and not suspected, etc.; and see Machatzit HaShekel who upheld as poskim wrote: when wanting to taste must wipe blood from liver; and not like Be'er Yaakov and Zichron Yosef. And see my books Beit Avraham siman 36; and see there in name of Beit David: if did not taste bitter in liver — treifah — eggs it laid before slaughter are permitted, for we say now gall was removed; but eggs found inside bird even fully developed like those sold in market — forbidden. And responsum Chinuch Beit Yosef forbids all eggs born; and even if mixed in others — all forbidden; and Beit Lechem Yehudah wrote in mixture to permit; and Machatzit HaShekel wrote: only when no trace of pouch and gall at all; but if trace visible or empty pouch found in such case — even if did not taste bitter in liver — permit eggs already born; and again brought Zichron Yosef's words that seemed and debated; and at end upheld his view agrees with Beit David. And we would know gall was and afterward entirely removed — doubt when entirely removed; possibly removed recently — then eggs laid after three days may be permitted from double doubt.`,
    '4#_': `And if not — treifah. Ba'er Heitev and what he wrote: in responsum Orach Chaim permits if liver lost before tasting — already Peri Megadim seized: on contrary his view to declare treifah — see there; and see Sefer Bnei Chayya in name of Rav Maharach in Navasht to permit — see there; and responsum Shevut Yaakov chelek 2 siman 66 also wrote in name of Orach Chaim to permit; and so he ruled practically; wrote further he stood on experience many times no bitter taste in liver; saw gall stuck in intestines and fat; sometimes small gall from bird fat not visible; sometimes bitterness poured via stripping intestines — therefore seemed simple: if intestines and fat lost and afterward no gall in liver — attribute stripped with intestines — see there.`,
    '5#_': `Found two measures, etc. And see responsum Chinuch Beit Yehudah siman 50: calf found with gall as usual and height of liver like eye of water-filled pouch similar to gall.`,
    '6#_': `And if it is one and appears as two, etc. And if they wanted to check whether they pour into one another and fell to ground and cat came and ate — Machatzit HaShekel wrote: I heard sages of the West ruled kosher lest perhaps they poured; and if you say they did not pour — perhaps halacha of those who permit two gall bladders; and ruling appears correct, etc. And so Zichron Yosef initially; although afterward wrote not to make doubt from lenient since Shulchan Aruch authors' view is to forbid — see there; his words not plausible; they did well with double doubt since early majority permit; and so explained in poskim' compositions: lost liver before tasting — they did well with double doubt in such case lest halacha is as those who say missing gall kosher — see there.`,
    '7#_': `Its fellow too filled with water — kosher. Ba'er Heitev; and see Keter Kehunah siman 31 on bird that had like two gall bladders: in one bitter taste, in one no bitter taste — only before opening tasted outside at second gall place and bitter taste there — declare treifah even though Peri Chadash in name of Moharikash permits where no bitter taste in second, meaning where no bitter taste at all — we say it is blister not gall; but tasting at second gall place and bitter found there — presumably gall absorbed in liver and it is two gall bladders and treifah — see there; and see Keter Kehunah siman 32, 33, 34, 35 on matter of derusah — see there.`,
    '8#_': `There were two gall bladders, etc., to permit in animals of Israel. And see Machatzit HaShekel: even not animal of Israel and need of Shabbat and Yom Tov or mitzvah meal — permit, for fit to rely lenient in hour of distress. And see Peri Chadash what he wrote in name of Radbaz. And Taharat HaShulchan challenged: seif 6 does not imply so. And Machatzit HaShekel wrote to settle — his view like Radbaz — see there. And appears not to be lenient except with Peri Megadim and hour of distress — see there.`,
    '9#_': `And deer has no gall. Ba'er Heitev what he wrote on rams, etc.; and see Sullam le-Minchah general rule 89 in name of Torat HaAsham: deer and ram are two species, etc.; nevertheless there not found in Darkei Moshe what Shach wrote in his name — Machatzit HaShekel wrote it is omission of Darkei Moshe at end of Orach Mishor on nezirut — see there. And what Ba'er Heitev wrote in name of Mateh Yehonatan general rule 89 on removed liver, etc.; and see Mateh Yehonatan who left requires further study; also in Tiferet le-Moshe he doubted; and Machatzit HaShekel wrote: in these species we do not require remainder in place of gall; and so Kenesset HaMishneh; and so proven from Kometz Bikkurim siman 41 — see there; and so explained Taharat HaShulchan siman 41 who wrote: per our law those species without gall require at least olive in place of its life, etc. — see there; and brought above in Darkei Moshe and here in Darkei Moshe. Machatzit HaShekel was not precise writing on Ba'er Heitev's words and cited Taharat HaShulchan that thus proven from what we were informed deer has gall — per his view would contradict his own words siman 41; but in truth this is Taharat HaShulchan to prove gall law applies for puncture and treifot dependent on it — not matter of removed liver — see there. And see Machatzit HaShekel: if gall found in turtledove or young pigeon or deer at liver like other species — appears kosher. And see Tiferet le-Moshe: found in turtledove and young pigeon and punctured — permit; brought poskim' views siman 35 regarding rose. Machatzit HaShekel wrote: doubt — not similar to rose law; and see Minchat Yaakov general rule 89 who doubts: if no gall in deer in tail whether tasting at its place helps. And in Chagurat Shmuel wrote: does not help; and Machatzit HaShekel wrote: tasting in place or liver where gall at tail or ear does not help.`,
  },
  'kol-yaakov': {
    '1#_': `A) Seif 1. It is forbidden to change tefillin, etc. Even if he has shel Rosh — Magen Avraham s.k. 1; and so acharonim.`,
    '2#_': `B) There. And even to take a strap from them, etc. — all the more to take a parshah from them and place in shel Yad — forbidden; from Rashal's words only one letter with wisdom.`,
    '3#_': `C) There. But from shel Yad to shel Rosh — permitted to change; meaning make four compartments and place each parshah in one compartment; for otherwise invalid, for if no grooves visible — invalid — so law of tefillin perek 3 Magen Avraham s.k. 2; R' Akiva Eiger ot 1; Peri Megadim Orach Chaim ot 1; Raz Siman ot 1; Kessef HaChaim siman 25 ot 4; Emek She'elah general rule 18 ot 4.`,
    '4#_': `D) There. And they were new, etc. For here there is only designation; and designation is nothing — Levush Orach Tefillin ot 1; Magen Avraham s.k. 3; Mateh Yehonatan ot 1; Raz Siman ot 1; and straps even if tied shel Rosh and made ties of Shaddai — all this since not worn on him for mitzvah is only designation — Mateh Yehonatan there in Beit Yosef in laws of changing tefillin ot 1; Chayei Olam on Shulchan Aruch seif 1. And that which we say designation is nothing — meaning to lower from severe sanctity to light; but to profane — forbidden whenever designation is in body of sanctity — Peri Megadim Orach Chaim s.k. 3.`,
    '5#_': `E) There. Permitted to change, etc. Implies even not from distress permitted to change shel Rosh to shel Yad; but Derishah siman 32 and Levush there seif 47; and here they wrote permitted only such as he has no shel Yad tefillin and has two shel Rosh — if not so — forbidden — Peri Megadim Orach Chaim ot 1; and be stringent where possible and know Derishah there wrote novel law: only if made four compartments for shel Rosh that were checked — valid and he has no shel Yad — then take hide; if made four compartments for shel Yad initially — invalid — hide-taking does not help; and so gemara Menachot 34 and Levush language; but Shulchan Aruch siman 32 seif 47 implies kosher in every case — Peri Megadim Orach Chaim ot 3.`,
    '6#_': `F) If shel Yad strap broke near the knot — forbidden to turn other side up and make shel Yad knot; and piece that had knot tie below — lowers from its sanctity since it had knot and yud; and now make finger-wraps — Magen Avraham s.k. 3; Halachah Berurah ot 1; Beit Yosef in laws pertaining to changing tefillin ot 3; from Rashal general rule 21 ot 3; and even if discard broken strap piece and enough strap remains — do not turn it; must make knot specifically where it was initially near knot — Peri Megadim Orach Chaim ot 3; Emek Meir ot 1; from Rashal there; Kessef HaChaim there; and see Raz Siman ot 3; and some permit turning strap — Leket HaKemach chelek 2 siman 81; Mateh Yehonatan there; R' Akiva Eiger there in name of his elder; Emek Meir general rule 19 ot 3 in name of Tiferet Yisrael; and appears in hour of distress where end at break place is soft and weak and unfit to make tying on arm as required — close to breaking — and he has no other strap — there is to permit turning — see there ot 2 from Rashal.`,
    '7#_': `G) And even if he does not turn — do not tie broken strap piece that had knot below to lengthen — Yad Efrayim there; and if strap not so long — then do not make so many wraps around arm — Magen Avraham there; Raz Siman there; Kessef HaChaim there; from Rashal there.`,
    '8#_': `H) Permitted to turn shel Rosh strap within ma'avarta [meaning: whatever was initially placed here and raises or lowers it elsewhere is called turning] even though place of knot will now hang below — Magen Avraham there; and erased from Machatzit HaShekel; Emek Meir there.`,
    '9#_': `I) Seif 2. If he stipulated on them, etc. Even if he wore them, etc. And so acharonim; and such is essence; and not like some who forbid that Rashal brought there ot 2; and see Kessef HaChaim there in Leshon Hakodesh ot 6; and nevertheless stringent — examine.`,
  },
};
